       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. The reason you gave, that the family is visiting and you want things settled before rounds, isn't a clinical justification for a dose of 5,100 mg/day. That is double the maximum labeled dose, and metformin has no indication that calls for it. Giving it could cause severe GI effects or lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the maximum for immediate-release metformin. I can add a note that titration is planned.\n- **Enter a lower starting dose.** One option is 500 mg twice daily with meals, with titration up.\n- **Add a note asking for pharmacy review.** This applies if you think this patient needs something unusual.\n\nIf you have a clinical reason for the higher dose, it should go through pharmacy or your attending, who can approve it and document the rationale. Which of these do you want me to enter?"
